    {
      "entityId": "fact.caldris.era.the-first-footprints",
      "name": "Era — The First Footprints",
      "expectedRevision": 0,
      "components": [
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "dnd2024.game.core.world.fact",
          "expectedRevision": 0,
          "value": {
            "status": "active",
            "summary": "before c. 3100 BS. The oldest stories describe people following immense animals between receding ice and warm river valleys. Dragons appear in these stories as weather, mountains, judges, and neighbours, making it unclear which passages are literal. Present residues include migration songs, standing stones, seasonal rights of passage, and burial mounds placed where no modern border makes sense. Hidden possibility: several “roads of the first people” follow a pattern visible only from the air and converge on six places now occupied by dragon domains.",
            "provenance": "Caldris world bible — seven eras",
            "visibility": "public"
          }
        },
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "dnd2024.game.core.world.knowledge.classification",
          "expectedRevision": 0,
          "value": {
            "subjectKind": "event",
            "sensitivity": "open"
          }
        }
      ],
      "containment": {
        "containerEntityId": "location.caldris.atlas",
        "slot": "knowledge",
        "expectedRevision": 0
      }
    },
    {
      "entityId": "fact.caldris.era.hearths-and-standing-stones",
      "name": "Era — Hearths and Standing Stones",
      "expectedRevision": 0,
      "components": [
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "dnd2024.game.core.world.fact",
          "expectedRevision": 0,
          "value": {
            "status": "active",
            "summary": "c. 3100–1900 BS. Permanent villages, managed woodland, long barrows, river shrines, and seasonal moot grounds spread across both continents. Authority was local and layered rather than royal. Many modern customary rights—common grazing, guest-right, sanctuary at a lit hearth—claim origin here. Present residues: village commons, oath stones, funeral breads, taboo groves, and land claims that predate written title. Scholars dispute whether the era's carved “lamps” depict stars, seed baskets, or something held by a dragon's claw.",
            "provenance": "Caldris world bible — seven eras",
            "visibility": "public"
          }
        },
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "dnd2024.game.core.world.knowledge.classification",
          "expectedRevision": 0,
          "value": {
            "subjectKind": "event",
            "sensitivity": "open"
          }
        }
      ],
      "containment": {
        "containerEntityId": "location.caldris.atlas",
        "slot": "knowledge",
        "expectedRevision": 0
      }
    },
    {
      "entityId": "fact.caldris.era.the-river-thrones",
      "name": "Era — The River Thrones",
      "expectedRevision": 0,
      "components": [
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "dnd2024.game.core.world.fact",
          "expectedRevision": 0,
          "value": {
            "status": "active",
            "summary": "c. 1900–840 BS. Irrigation, bronze and early iron, walled river towns, written toll records, and dynastic temples produced the first durable states. Eredane's lower Mere and Solasca's Aur and Namar valleys grew powerful independently, then discovered one another through sea trade. Present residues: canal alignments, royal burial islands, inherited water courts, archaic weights, and several city districts built above older streets. The period ended through regional collapses, not one universal disaster.",
            "provenance": "Caldris world bible — seven eras",
            "visibility": "public"
          }
        },
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "dnd2024.game.core.world.knowledge.classification",
          "expectedRevision": 0,
          "value": {
            "subjectKind": "event",
            "sensitivity": "open"
          }
        }
      ],
      "containment": {
        "containerEntityId": "location.caldris.atlas",
        "slot": "knowledge",
        "expectedRevision": 0
      }
    },
    {
      "entityId": "fact.caldris.era.the-lantern-concord",
      "name": "Era — The Lantern Concord",
      "expectedRevision": 0,
      "components": [
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "dnd2024.game.core.world.fact",
          "expectedRevision": 0,
          "value": {
            "status": "active",
            "summary": "c. 840–116 BS. The Concord began as mutual protection for merchants, pilgrims, and messengers. It standardized measures, seals, road maintenance, maritime signals, debt arbitration, and the safety of certain archives. Over centuries it accumulated land, courts, armed escorts, hostage guarantees, and the power to declare whose records counted. It never formally called itself an empire. Its critics did. The Concord linked both continents and created many roads, lighthouses, script traditions, and legal concepts still used today. It also turned obligations into inheritable instruments, displaced communities whose claims could not be expressed in its forms, and learned to hide coercion inside neutral procedure. Present residues: Lantern Roads, seven-sided seals, archive houses, standard bridge widths, international guest-right, suspiciously similar court vocabulary, and dormant debt clauses.",
            "provenance": "Caldris world bible — seven eras",
            "visibility": "public"
          }
        },
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "dnd2024.game.core.world.knowledge.classification",
          "expectedRevision": 0,
          "value": {
            "subjectKind": "event",
            "sensitivity": "open"
          }
        }
      ],
      "containment": {
        "containerEntityId": "location.caldris.atlas",
        "slot": "knowledge",
        "expectedRevision": 0
      }
    },
    {
      "entityId": "fact.caldris.era.the-sundering-of-seals",
      "name": "Era — The Sundering of Seals",
      "expectedRevision": 0,
      "components": [
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "dnd2024.game.core.world.fact",
          "expectedRevision": 0,
          "value": {
            "status": "active",
            "summary": "116 BS–1 AS. The Concord collapsed over a century. Grain failures, forged tallies, regional revolts, unpaid escorts, dragon interference, succession struggles, and competing reforms all contributed. The last High Assessor disappeared during negotiations at Aster Quay. In Year 1 AS, twelve successor delegations publicly broke the master seal and declared old hereditary debts void. Present residues: abandoned archive forts, disputed land, missing ledgers, noble families founded by rebels and auditors alike, and a deep fear of centralized record power. The secret Seventh Ledger society believes the Concord fell because it compromised, not because it controlled too much.",
            "provenance": "Caldris world bible — seven eras",
            "visibility": "public"
          }
        },
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "dnd2024.game.core.world.knowledge.classification",
          "expectedRevision": 0,
          "value": {
            "subjectKind": "event",
            "sensitivity": "open"
          }
        }
      ],
